00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:25.710
OK, so from an abstraction
layer, what you're getting

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:29.460
is a control plane and
a data plane, right?

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:34.200
So the data plane is
the multi-tenancy layer,

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:38.710
which really means
that different users

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:41.640
from different business groups
or different development teams

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.210
can go to the interface
of the control plane

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:48.450
to deploy their own tenants, OK?

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:53.040
So the tenants are color-coded
by blue and green, right?

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.400
So you can see that there's
kind of a separation as far

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.360
as the default services
that run on the tenants

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:03.990
as well as services that
run on the control plane.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:06.810
So the control plane
itself runs Kubernetes,

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:11.490
so it's kind of like Kubernetes
deploying Kubernetes, right?

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:14.430
And below that
abstraction layer is just

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:16.920
the physical layer,
which is the storage,

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.810
the hypervisor, the networking.

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:25.200
So we, very similar to a lot
of solutions in the industry,

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.560
are using Prometheus
for monitoring,

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.010
Kibana as ELK stack.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:35.070
And then, we're
also using Grafana

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:38.669
to visualize some of the
performance monitoring

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:42.510
for Kubernetes.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:45.792
So architecturally,
and both from a logical

00:08:45.792 --> 00:08:48.250
and a physical perspective, it
would look like this, right?

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.370
So you will have--

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:52.260
HX stands for HyperFlex.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:55.150
So those basically would
be your compute nodes.

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:57.840
So on your compute
nodes, if you have

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.130
a cluster of three compute
nodes, you would have compute,

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:05.370
and then you would have VMs
that run the control plane that

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:07.530
would be the blue VMs, right?

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:11.370
So the blue VMs obviously would
have the same connectivity

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:15.180
into, let's say, your
storage, your layer 3 network,

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:16.200
your core networking.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.030
All of those things apply.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:22.800
And then you have the green,
which represents the tenant

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:24.360
nodes or tenant clusters.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.490
Those are also VMs, right?

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:30.930
And of course, you can have a
one-to-many, meaning many VMs

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:35.937
operating on one physical
node, which is the HX server.

00:09:35.937 --> 00:09:37.770
And then, of course,
what we're showing here

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:44.040
is that it's a well-connected
stack, whether you're

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.711
talking about the physical or
the virtualized environment,

00:09:47.711 --> 00:09:48.210
right?

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:50.040
And it's self-enclosed.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:56.981
It has access to a 10 to 40-gig
bandwidth on the network layer,

00:09:56.981 --> 00:09:57.480
right?

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:59.560
And then upstream, you can--

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:05.040
well, and you can do underlay
or overlay from a Kubernetes

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:07.000
cluster point of view.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.760
And then, of course,
it will integrate

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.580
into your upstream layer 3
or L3 [INAUDIBLE] gateways.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:15.910
Hopefully, that makes sense.

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:19.290
So one thing about the PGs,
which stands for Port Group,

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:20.100
and VLANs--

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:21.390
