00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:28.310
OK, so now we're going
to talk about storage,

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:30.020
and we're going to
address specifically

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:34.880
why HyperFlex, the
hyperconverged platform

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:40.640
that Cisco sells, is
important in this relationship

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:41.930
with Kubernetes, right?

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:47.180
So you probably are aware that
within Kubernetes, there's

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:51.390
kind of a native driver
approach to provisioning

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:55.400
PVs or Persistent Volumes,
which means that you can

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.190
use the basic native driver.

00:13:58.190 --> 00:13:59.880
It's nothing fancy.

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:06.380
It just delivers a
volume to the containers.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:13.880
But if you need to deliver a
high I/O persistent volume that

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:17.150
also can have some
special attributes,

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:20.630
then you'd move into the
FlexVolume framework, right?

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:23.330
So this FlexVolume
framework allows

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:28.130
manufacturers to create
their own specialized plugins

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:29.480
for Kubernetes.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:34.550
So that all just described
the FlexVolume driver that

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:39.035
is specific to the HX platform.

00:14:39.035 --> 00:14:42.491
OK, and then I'm going to
go through how it actually

00:14:42.491 --> 00:14:44.240
works, although I'm
not going to really go

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:45.740
through the deep dive, right?

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:51.170
This is just-- for instance,
if you have an ESX server--

00:14:51.170 --> 00:14:54.020
that's the middle
green layer, right?

00:14:54.020 --> 00:15:01.250
So by default, the
abstraction layer of HX

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:07.880
allows for NFS data stores to
be presented to ESX servers, OK?

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:12.080
Now that is just
typical shared storage.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:17.000
The VMs that are deployed
through CCP, which are

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:19.880
the Kubernetes cluster nodes--

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:23.510
they can both access
the NFS, which

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:29.300
is just file storage or
shared volumes, and then

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:34.940
they can also, via
iSCSI, via the IO Visor--

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:37.190
they can access iSCSI volumes.

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:40.940
Now, the important thing about
them accessing iSCSI volumes

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:49.880
is you are mounting an I/O
level volume to the container

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:53.570
so that the container can-- it's
a block level device, right?

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:57.260
So the container can
actually format that volume

00:15:57.260 --> 00:15:59.690
and can do special things to it.

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:03.740
And the benefit--
yeah, so the benefit

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:07.970
is that on HX, because
it is hyperconverged,

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:13.490
the blocks of storage get stored
onto different servers, right?

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:20.221
This is very basic to how HX
delivers a storage replication,

00:16:20.221 --> 00:16:20.720
right?

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:23.780
And then, of course, it also
automatically replicates--

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:25.610
you don't have to do this--

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:29.940
the blocks of storage
on different nodes.

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:34.400
So it creates a redundancy
so that if one node fails

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:38.480
or if one ESX server fails,
then Kubernetes will bring up

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:39.620
the containers, obviously.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:43.130
And then, the
containers will also

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:46.070
be remapped to their volumes.

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:55.520
OK, so the benefits of
doing this with HX and CCP

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:59.490
is that you'll be able to apply
some data retention policies,

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:01.230
data protection policies.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:04.250
And of course, you've got
automatic replication,

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:06.920
so that's inherent
within the platform,

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:10.520
and it's something that
is native to Kubernetes.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:12.440
You don't have to
do anything special.

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:16.250
You won't have to install
special drivers because

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:18.290
of the integration with CCP.

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:22.790
A CCP node will automatically
have the FlexVolume driver

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:27.779
installed every time you deploy
the tenant cluster from CCP.

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:35.180
And then, from a
networking perspective,

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:38.090
there are many different
options, underlay,

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:43.310
overlay options when you
talk about Kubernetes.

00:17:43.310 --> 00:17:46.820
Not all of these options
are standardized.

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:50.170
Some of them are pervasive
across the different clouds,

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:56.120
but the unified approach
to policy design, policy

00:17:56.120 --> 00:18:00.230
enforcement is one of the
challenges, and certainly

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:05.750
a challenge that we
are solving with CCP.

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:13.250
So there are three options for
CNIs when you talk about CCP.

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:16.820
I'm going to go into the
first and the second option.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:18.170
The third option is Calico.

00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:19.490
That's the pervasive one.

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:21.110
You can find it on GKE.

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:24.740
You can find it on
AKS and EKS also.

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:30.320
So it's something
that certainly you

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:32.870
can do research on your own.

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:37.440
The first two are the ones
that I'll dive deep into.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:42.395
So if you aren't
familiar with ACI,

00:18:42.395 --> 00:18:45.600
ACI is our Application-Centric
Infrastructure,

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:51.140
which is really just a fancy
name for an SDN solution

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:54.390
that is both hardware
and software.

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:57.380
So what I'm describing
is the ability for, let's

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:01.550
say, a network administrator
or a network operator

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:06.650
to design policies that can
be enforced by the software

00:19:06.650 --> 00:19:08.180
or by the hardware, right?

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:11.370
So it's designed in software,
applied at hardware,

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:15.440
and it's effective to
east-west communication, right?

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:18.080
So north-south--
east-west is really

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:21.230
where microservices
need protection,

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:26.990
and ACI is that for both
microservices and VM-based

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:27.710
deployments.

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:33.300
So this is OVS based.

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:37.170
I believe that it's a
derivative of OpenDaylight.

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:42.300
Cisco has had some integration
with OVS in the past.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:44.640
This integration-- the
ACI-CNI integration--

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:49.350
is a little different, but
there are certain steps

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:53.940
to do this if you had a
Kubernetes cluster that was not

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:56.250
deployed by CCP.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:59.400
So you have the ability
to do this on your own

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:02.010
if you have Kubernetes and ACI.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:06.270
What CCP is doing
is it is automating

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:08.670
all of the integration
points for you.

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:10.260
So there are a few
things that you

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:13.770
have to enter into
configurations,

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:18.870
and then there is YAML
files that you have to bind.

00:20:18.870 --> 00:20:23.360
The CCP integration will do
all of that automatically,

00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:31.380
and it's beneficial to both the
application architects as well

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:34.950
as the network administrators
because it's dynamic, right?

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:38.790
It automatically
protects the pods

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:40.410
that are running in the fabric.

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:47.690
There are three different
models of protection.

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:51.690
So the default model
is a cluster isolation,

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:57.650
which means that if
you've got a pod subnet,

00:20:57.650 --> 00:21:01.220
all of the pods
across the clusters

00:21:01.220 --> 00:21:04.640
are protected as
one entity, right?

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:10.730
So in ACI, it's called an
EPG or an End Point Group.

00:21:10.730 --> 00:21:15.800
The pods would just be
considered endpoints, right?

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:18.050
And then, they
would be protected

00:21:18.050 --> 00:21:22.700
as one, singular unit
inside the cluster.

00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:25.970
There's also-- if you
wanted to annotate

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:29.810
the different
namespaces, then you

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:33.600
could accomplish a
namespace isolation,

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:35.510
which is not a cluster-wide.

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:37.580
Then it would be a
kind of separation

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:42.290
at the namespace layer, which
is pretty unique when you think

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:47.090
about it because things
that operate inside

00:21:47.090 --> 00:21:49.610
of a namespace or microservices
that are running inside

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:54.020
of a namespace can be
protected both from each other

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:57.590
as far as the workloads, and
then from other namespaces,

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:58.130
right?

00:21:58.130 --> 00:22:01.850
So this is treating
pods as endpoints

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:04.310
on the network fabric.

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:06.710
And then, of course,
you can go as granular

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:09.390
as protecting the
deployments themselves.

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:12.890
So each deployment, each
replica of the deployment

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:16.391
would be protected by other
services in the deployment.

00:22:16.391 --> 00:22:17.640
So that's very, very granular.

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:25.600
And then, ACI, from a
visualization construct,

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:27.610
makes it easy for
network administrators

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:31.150
to see what is happening
and to also operate

00:22:31.150 --> 00:22:32.950
the things that
may be a little bit

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:39.130
difficult for the application
developers to express, right?

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:43.480
So we don't expect
application developers

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:46.710
to understand the
network fabric the way

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:48.460
that a network
administrator would, right?

00:22:48.460 --> 00:22:52.120
But if there's a way for us to
visualize it, which there is--

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:55.060
a way for us to
visualize the filters

00:22:55.060 --> 00:23:00.280
and the different contracts
and the relationship

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:02.920
that we establish
in ACI between what

00:23:02.920 --> 00:23:04.970
we call the consumer
and the provider,

00:23:04.970 --> 00:23:07.780
which is source and
destination, then

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:12.760
we make it easy for the teams to
collaborate and to troubleshoot

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:14.110
in one of those firestorms.

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:21.790
OK, so one of the other CNI
options, probably something

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:23.280
you may have heard of--

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:27.520
it's really a
networking platform

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:30.860
that is utilized by
service providers.

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:32.380
It's called Contiv.

00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:36.970
It's open source, and it's been
inside of Cisco for a while.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:39.520
Our service provider
customers use it,

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:43.720
but it's basically a user space.

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:47.170
It's a networking platform
that bypasses the kernel,

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:52.510
uses the user space, which means
that it depends on the driver

00:23:52.510 --> 00:23:57.280
in order to propagate bandwidth
across the different nodes,

00:23:57.280 --> 00:23:58.330
right?

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:02.020
So it's optimized because it's
not serial packet processing.

00:24:02.020 --> 00:24:05.590
It's vector packet
processing, and it's something

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:07.840
that you can
probably get a deeper

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:13.600
dive in if you're tied to
a Cisco account or a Cisco

00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:14.890
engineer.

00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:19.600
It's, I think, a very
intuitive way to deal

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:22.007
with container networking.

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:32.340
OK, so we have a plan to
really follow the release

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:34.920
cycle of Kubernetes itself.

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:39.210
So we expect that
every major release--

00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:41.370
1 to 2, 2 to 3--

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:46.720
is going to be very aligned
to Kubernetes release cycles.

00:24:46.720 --> 00:24:50.160
We're planning on
that to be quarterly,

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:56.920
patching upgrades, which is
X.X, is going to be on demand.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:25:00.590
And then minor releases
will be monthly.

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:05.850
So we have right now a 1.3
that was just released.

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:08.790
1.4 will come out next month.

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:11.130
2.0 will come out
the next quarter.

00:25:11.130 --> 00:25:14.475
So that's kind of our
plan for release cycles.

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:21.500
OK, and then obviously, we'll
have a compatibility matrix

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:24.500
of the different
parts of the ecosystem

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:27.110
that we're going
to integrate into,

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:31.940
which is a great segue into
the Hybrid Cloud Platform.

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:33.600
You may have heard about this.

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:37.450
You may have also heard
about the GKE On-Prem.

00:25:37.450 --> 00:25:44.020
So the thing that we
are really focused on

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:49.990
as far as the Hybrid Cloud
Platform is the hybrid cloud

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:52.510
deployment and lifecycle
management, right?

00:25:52.510 --> 00:25:55.840
So we know that
there are new roles--

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:59.740
DevOps, service
designers, people

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:02.680
who work on modeling
their consumption.

00:26:02.680 --> 00:26:05.140
All those different roles and
different responsibilities--

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:08.980
they need to come
into a tool or a tool

00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:11.800
set that they can
all relate to, right?

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:14.950
So all of their
roles are converging.

00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:20.110
And of course, the environments
are expanding and also getting

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:23.290
more complex, especially when
you talk about integrating

00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:27.230
those different environments.

00:26:27.230 --> 00:26:31.390
So the way that I've been
describing the Hyper Cloud

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:34.720
Platform is it is a reference
architecture, right?

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:37.690
So it's very much like FlexPod,
if you've heard about FlexPods

00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:38.830
in the past.

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:41.440
It's something that you
could build yourself.

00:26:41.440 --> 00:26:45.100
What we're doing is we are
building it, integrating it,

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:48.380
and maintaining the
compatibility matrix for you,

00:26:48.380 --> 00:26:48.880
right?

00:26:48.880 --> 00:26:51.050
So we're developing
best practices.

00:26:51.050 --> 00:26:53.890
What this is is a
reference architecture that

00:26:53.890 --> 00:26:58.850
gets validated designs, that
gets updated every quarter,

00:26:58.850 --> 00:27:05.110
that you can either use the
validated design to build it

00:27:05.110 --> 00:27:08.290
out yourself in a few
months, or if you wanted

00:27:08.290 --> 00:27:10.750
to engage a partner, we've
got plenty of partners

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:12.880
that have worked with us.

00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:18.550
And we've also run an EAP--

00:27:18.550 --> 00:27:20.110
an Early Access Program--

00:27:20.110 --> 00:27:22.330
for this very architecture.

00:27:22.330 --> 00:27:25.760
So what that means is that
in the past several months,

00:27:25.760 --> 00:27:29.380
we've placed this
architecture-- so rack and stack

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:33.720
and configuration-- into
data centers for eight

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:35.860
of our strategic
partners, right?

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:38.860
And then we've had them go
through workbook exercises

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:43.300
and validate the solution
stack as well as the use cases

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:44.800
that we're solving, right?

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:48.250
So this has already gone
through validation cycles.

00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:54.220
I'd say that we're onto our
second phase of the validation.

00:27:54.220 --> 00:27:57.130
But the reference
architecture is

00:27:57.130 --> 00:28:01.930
going to provide to you
a consistent environment

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:04.930
and a consistent
model for deploying

00:28:04.930 --> 00:28:07.900
as well as for
designing policies

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:11.440
and governance for both
the enterprise as well

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:12.370
as the public cloud.

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:18.660
OK, and then this is
one of the customers

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:20.240
that we've worked
very closely with.

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:24.660
So I'm sure that all of
you have heard about SAP.

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:28.560
They have a heritage in
data warehousing on prem.

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:31.080
They were seeing
that data definitely

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:35.700
was being distributed,
especially IoT data or sensor

00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:40.260
data that was being
processed by ML, or Machine

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:44.010
Learning, or just big
data in the cloud, right?

00:28:44.010 --> 00:28:47.880
So there was a preferential
treatment for customer or sales

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:50.722
data that had to exist on prem.

00:28:50.722 --> 00:28:52.680
And then, of course, it
needed to be integrated

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:57.130
with IoT data that existed, of
course, on the public cloud--

00:28:57.130 --> 00:28:58.440
in the public cloud.

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:01.350
And so they created
this data hub,

00:29:01.350 --> 00:29:07.920
which basically has a serverless
data pipelining component

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:09.210
to the framework.

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:11.520
And then it has a
runtime that has

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:15.120
to be delivered near
where the data is.

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:17.790
So that's kind of a
short summary of what

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:19.860
they were doing with data hub.

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:23.070
But the reason
why they partnered

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:27.730
with us is because
the data hub and then

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:29.430
the framework for
the data hub needed

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:31.650
to run on Kubernetes, right?

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:37.470
And so Kubernetes-- the data
hub itself needed to be on prem,

00:29:37.470 --> 00:29:41.910
and we delivered CCP for
them to meet this demand.

00:29:41.910 --> 00:29:44.790
And so you can see that they
chose Kubernetes because one,

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:47.220
containers can be pervasive
across the different cloud

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:48.490
platforms.

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:50.790
And that's their
approach, is they

00:29:50.790 --> 00:29:54.670
need interconnectivity between
the different cloud targets,

00:29:54.670 --> 00:29:55.170
right?

00:29:55.170 --> 00:30:01.890
And they need a way to
provide secure transport

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:08.730
as well as just connectivity
and overall policy for the pods

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:11.580
that run.

00:30:11.580 --> 00:30:16.930
OK, still time to visit
us at the Cisco booth,

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:20.890
and then many ways for
you to engage with us.

00:30:20.890 --> 00:30:26.865
I'm going to have time for
a demo, so it's very good.

00:30:26.865 --> 00:30:28.990
And then, I don't know if
you heard about the Cloud

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:29.698
Challenge, right?

00:30:29.698 --> 00:30:33.220
So if you are a
part of a team that

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:36.520
needs immediate and
impactful results,

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:39.190
you can enter into
the challenge.

00:30:39.190 --> 00:30:43.150
Other than bragging rights,
you can win $25,000 of credits

00:30:43.150 --> 00:30:46.120
in GCP, which is a grand sum.

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:50.530
And then you can get a
CCP license for a year.

00:30:50.530 --> 00:30:54.320
OK, so I'm going to
go into the demo, so--

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:59.510
OK, so what I'm going to
show you first is CCP,

00:30:59.510 --> 00:31:02.900
and you can see that I've
got tenant clusters already

00:31:02.900 --> 00:31:04.530
deployed.

00:31:04.530 --> 00:31:08.570
Now, let's make sure--

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:11.860
because I mentioned that
there is a VMware component,

00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:14.360
I'm going to open
up VMware, and we'll

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:16.820
see if we can
visualize that for you.

00:31:16.820 --> 00:31:26.690
But one of the things
that is unique to CCP

00:31:26.690 --> 00:31:29.930
is that we do upgrades, right?

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:31.311
So it's automated.

00:31:31.311 --> 00:31:32.810
You don't have to
get in line to it.

00:31:32.810 --> 00:31:36.200
You basically choose what it
is that you want to upgrade,

00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:39.410
whether it's the control plane
itself or the tenant plane.

00:31:39.410 --> 00:31:43.580
And then we do the
automation for it, right?

00:31:43.580 --> 00:31:45.560
So the reason why
I mention upgrades

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:49.490
is because all of us who have
interacted with Kubernetes

00:31:49.490 --> 00:31:51.275
knows that when you--

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:57.710
kubelet does not
suffer an IP change.

00:31:57.710 --> 00:31:58.490
It just doesn't.

00:31:58.490 --> 00:31:59.990
It doesn't suffer
through it, right?

00:31:59.990 --> 00:32:01.880
So if you change
the IP, then you'll

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:04.970
have issues with your
upgrade because the API

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:06.110
server won't come up.

00:32:06.110 --> 00:32:12.130
So the way that we deal with
this is to provide a VIP IP,

00:32:12.130 --> 00:32:12.630
right?

00:32:12.630 --> 00:32:16.920
So the VIP IP is assigned
to an API server,

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:21.260
and then, the attributes
of the existing cluster

00:32:21.260 --> 00:32:24.360
are going to be retained
when you upgrade, right?

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:28.010
So we give network
administrators and operators

00:32:28.010 --> 00:32:34.030
the ability to define their own
networks and their own pools.

00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:38.770
And then, I mentioned
that we today

00:32:38.770 --> 00:32:41.110
have integration into VMware.

00:32:41.110 --> 00:32:43.300
That's our
infrastructure provider.

00:32:43.300 --> 00:32:46.710
We're going to make this
compatible with OpenStack

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:50.090
and also bare metal
in the future.

00:32:50.090 --> 00:32:54.320
And then, as far as Active
Directory is concerned-- so

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:57.530
we assume that our
enterprise customers already

00:32:57.530 --> 00:33:00.600
have an existing
authentication provider.

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:04.250
So you'll see that this
is already authenticated.

00:33:04.250 --> 00:33:06.950
And then, all you
need really here

00:33:06.950 --> 00:33:10.010
is just a distinguished name.

00:33:10.010 --> 00:33:15.170
And of course, it runs
over TLS securely.

00:33:15.170 --> 00:33:20.160
And then, you define
which AD groups you want.

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:24.300
And then, we automatically query
those AD groups for the users,

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:27.890
and we allow them to
log into CCP, right?

00:33:27.890 --> 00:33:32.580
So probably, this is kind of
one of those table stakes.

00:33:32.580 --> 00:33:37.220
But certainly, SSO and
mutual authentication

00:33:37.220 --> 00:33:40.610
and just integration into
an existing authentication

00:33:40.610 --> 00:33:44.690
provider is something
that is challenging

00:33:44.690 --> 00:33:49.780
for doing Kubernetes manually,
and then doing it consistently.

00:33:52.570 --> 00:33:55.570
So we'll walk through
that three-step process

00:33:55.570 --> 00:33:57.730
that I was talking about, right?

00:33:57.730 --> 00:34:01.540
And we actually won't
deploy a cluster

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:04.360
because I'm going to do
it with an API, right?

00:34:14.389 --> 00:34:17.260
OK, so here, we're
basically choosing

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:23.909
the attributes of a
typical VMware environment.

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:27.920
And the thing about
the networking layer

00:34:27.920 --> 00:34:30.040
is that you can choose--

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:32.960
let's say the Contiv CNI--

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:36.560
and you can still operate
the Contiv CNI cluster

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:38.960
inside of an ACI fabric.

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:40.460
So there are a lot of choices.

00:34:40.460 --> 00:34:44.150
It just depends on the
underlay and overlay options

00:34:44.150 --> 00:34:47.179
that you want to choose.

00:34:47.179 --> 00:34:49.850
And then, all I'm
walking through here

00:34:49.850 --> 00:34:51.760
is just the different
options that you have.

00:34:54.360 --> 00:34:57.650
So we're going to
keep this standard.

00:34:57.650 --> 00:35:00.860
And then, the reason
for the username

00:35:00.860 --> 00:35:02.450
is because we'll inject--

00:35:02.450 --> 00:35:04.910
we'll create the user
name and inject the key

00:35:04.910 --> 00:35:09.140
so that when the users
want to directly interact

00:35:09.140 --> 00:35:15.530
with the nodes, it's going to be
delivered by an SSH key and not

00:35:15.530 --> 00:35:16.730
a password.

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:22.400
All right, so you get
all these options.

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:24.280
And then, this is basically--

00:35:24.280 --> 00:35:26.184
that was the three
screens, and this would

00:35:26.184 --> 00:35:27.350
be the fourth screen, right?

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:29.870
So what I'm describing
to you is a reduction

00:35:29.870 --> 00:35:38.630
of the complicated logic that
initially would apply to doing

00:35:38.630 --> 00:35:40.400
Kubernetes on your own, right?

00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:44.960
Now, imagine that you can
offer this as service catalog

00:35:44.960 --> 00:35:46.730
to multiple users.

00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:49.790
When you do this, obviously you
can apply a governance model

00:35:49.790 --> 00:35:52.860
around that, right?

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:54.430
OK, so I'm going to go back.

00:35:58.040 --> 00:36:02.851
And let's say that I want to
interact with this cluster,

00:36:02.851 --> 00:36:03.350
right?

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:05.800
So I have the option
to upgrade, but I

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:09.522
won't upgrade because I don't
have the tenant image for that.

00:36:09.522 --> 00:36:10.855
I'm going to download the token.

00:36:13.732 --> 00:36:18.020
OK, so we'll see here that's
the token name, right?

00:36:21.230 --> 00:36:25.560
OK, so what I'm going to do is--

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:29.690
all right, so basically
what I did was--

00:36:29.690 --> 00:36:32.390
in order for me to
interact with my cluster,

00:36:32.390 --> 00:36:34.320
I have to run kubectl, right?

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:36.290
In order for me to
run kubectl, have

00:36:36.290 --> 00:36:38.600
to define at least
a config file,

00:36:38.600 --> 00:36:41.270
and this is basically
that config file, right?

00:36:41.270 --> 00:36:45.710
So this is the representation
of that cluster

00:36:45.710 --> 00:36:49.310
that I saw in the UI.

00:36:49.310 --> 00:36:54.530
So what I'm showing you
is a seamless and easy way

00:36:54.530 --> 00:36:58.610
for consumers as well
as administrators

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:03.650
to interact with the
assets that they deploy.

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:09.960
OK, so I'll show
you that these are

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:12.360
all the different namespaces.

00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:13.725
And if we take a look at this--

00:37:16.640 --> 00:37:18.620
I may be going through
this too quickly,

00:37:18.620 --> 00:37:20.270
but it's basically
the same cluster.

00:37:20.270 --> 00:37:22.980
That's what I'm proving to you.

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:26.430
Every control plane has a
Swagger interface, right?

00:37:26.430 --> 00:37:30.090
So this is kind of the
design-first approach

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:36.220
to providing a
consumable platform.

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:42.730
So you can look at all the
different operations, right?

00:37:42.730 --> 00:37:48.780
And let's open it up.

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:51.690
And if you haven't
used Postman before,

00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:56.994
it's just a tool that allows
you to interact with APIs.

00:37:56.994 --> 00:37:58.160
So I'm going to get a token.

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:07.560
I'm going to do a
get for the clusters,

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:10.200
and then I see all the
different clusters.

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:14.290
So I'm going to do
a Create Cluster.

00:38:14.290 --> 00:38:16.350
All right, so hopefully
you can see that I'm

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:19.650
naming it Google Next 18 TBN.

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:26.170
So we do not have a
cluster with such name.

00:38:26.170 --> 00:38:31.980
And now, hopefully we'll see
that it starts to show up.

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:34.320
So it's going to take a
while to provision, more time

00:38:34.320 --> 00:38:35.850
than we have.

00:38:35.850 --> 00:38:40.260
But the point here is that you
can easily interact with it

00:38:40.260 --> 00:38:42.910
through APIs, all right?

00:38:42.910 --> 00:38:45.790
So one of the things that I
wanted to show very quickly--

00:38:45.790 --> 00:38:49.210
now again, this is the
Cloud Center product that

00:38:49.210 --> 00:38:54.190
is one of the pillars in
the Hybrid Cloud Platform--

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:57.522
is not a part of CCP, right?

00:38:57.522 --> 00:38:59.980
So I just want to make it clear
that what I'm showing you--

00:38:59.980 --> 00:39:03.340
Cloud Center-- is not
a component of CCP,

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:06.810
but it'll show you how--

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:11.320
it's one thing for a
developer or someone

00:39:11.320 --> 00:39:14.980
to just interact with the
cluster using kubectl, right?

00:39:14.980 --> 00:39:17.650
Very, very difficult for
you to apply a governance

00:39:17.650 --> 00:39:20.730
model around it.

00:39:20.730 --> 00:39:22.870
I won't let this
distract us too much.

00:39:22.870 --> 00:39:24.660
So basically, what
I was going to show

00:39:24.660 --> 00:39:31.020
you is that Cloud Center is
a multi-cloud application

00:39:31.020 --> 00:39:34.900
delivery and application
lifecycle management tool,

00:39:34.900 --> 00:39:35.400
right?

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:36.858
So what I was going
to show you is,

00:39:36.858 --> 00:39:43.410
hey, instead of allowing
users to just go to Kubernetes

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:46.680
and do a kubectl command
to add a new namespace

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:49.650
or to delete a namespace
or to create Istio

00:39:49.650 --> 00:39:53.946
or to install the
service catalog,

00:39:53.946 --> 00:39:57.690
you would develop
these different service

00:39:57.690 --> 00:39:58.890
catalog items, right?

00:39:58.890 --> 00:40:00.990
So you would present
them in the catalog,

00:40:00.990 --> 00:40:04.770
and then what you could
do is apply an RBAC model

00:40:04.770 --> 00:40:07.440
to the privileges to
those assets, right?

00:40:07.440 --> 00:40:10.410
So only certain people would
be able to do it, right?

00:40:10.410 --> 00:40:15.840
And of course, because of Cloud
Center's agnostic nature, what

00:40:15.840 --> 00:40:19.560
happens is you only have to
develop that service once,

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:20.130
right?

00:40:20.130 --> 00:40:22.260
And then, that
service conceivably

00:40:22.260 --> 00:40:25.140
could be executed on
any of the environments

00:40:25.140 --> 00:40:26.880
that Cloud Center
supports, right?

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:28.588
And if you don't know
about Cloud Center,

00:40:28.588 --> 00:40:31.440
it's quite broad in--

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:34.080
it has a very rich
integration ecosystem

00:40:34.080 --> 00:40:35.454
as far as clouds are concerned.

00:40:35.454 --> 00:40:36.870
All the public
clouds that you can

00:40:36.870 --> 00:40:39.780
think of, as well as
many OpenStack variants--

00:40:39.780 --> 00:40:41.610
and of course, we showed VMware.

